  <h1>What Is Colloidal Gold?</h1>
  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Colloidal gold is a suspension of gold nanoparticles (typically ~40 nm) that produces the visible red line in lateral-flow rapid tests. Antibodies are conjugated to the gold particles, which then serve as the colored signal.</p>

  <h2>Why gold</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Visible</strong> — gold nanoparticles appear red at test concentrations, readable by eye.</li>
    <li><strong>Stable</strong> — gold is inert and stable at room temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Conjugatable</strong> — antibodies adsorb onto gold particles by electrostatic interaction.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it produces the result</h2>
  <p>Gold-labeled antibodies bind the target analyte; the labeled complex is captured at the test line, where accumulated gold particles form a visible red line. Line intensity reflects the amount of captured label.</p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>Colloidal gold is why lateral-flow tests need no instrument and no cold chain — a key advantage for tropical and remote distribution. Particle size and conjugation quality directly affect sensitivity and line clarity.</p>

